On Thu, 2025-02-13 at 18:28 -0800, lyh...@gmail.com wrote:
>
> Dear Havard，
> Is there any toy example to show the coding of
>
> a * field.1 + (1-a) * field.2
> or
> s * ( \sqrt{a} * field.1 + \sqrt{(1-a)} * field.2) (*)
>
> in R-inla?
> It is quite interesting.
>
> Thank you very much
> C. LING
>
> 在2025年1月30日星期四 UTC+8 12:26:07<Helpdesk (Haavard Rue)> 写道：
> >
> > As far as I understand you, then you have replicated measurement of the two
> > fields over years, then you could write them as
> >
> >  a * field.1 + (1-a) * field.2
> > or
> >  s * ( \sqrt{a} * field.1 + \sqrt{(1-a)} * field.2) (*)
> >
> > as you see, then the variances of field.1 and 2 are confounded with 'a',
> > hence
> > this can be achived with
> >
> >  field.1 + field.2
> >
> > and the 'a' follows from the variances. the (*) representation would require
> > that the variances of field.1 and 2 are fixed, like = 1, then s^2 is the
> > overall
> > variances, and 'a' in (*) is the variance particion. (see
> > https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.16057 for a recent treatment of variance
> > particion)
> >
> >
> >
> >
> > On Wed, 2025-01-29 at 14:24 -0800, Palo wrote:
> > > Hello!
> > > I am trying to fit a spatial model using two spatial fields. In essence, I
> > > am
> > > assuming that there are two underlying spatial "states", and each year in
> > > my
> > > dataset is a linear combination of these two states. A crude
> > > representation of
> > > what I want to do would be:
> > >
> > > formula <- obs ~ -1 +
> > > year : f(spatial.field1, model = spde1_mod4) +
> > > year : f(spatial.field2, model = spde2_mod4)
> > >
> > > Though I am aware that I cannot specify the interaction like this. I'm not
> > > sure if I can use the 'group' argument, since I don't want the actual
> > > spatial
> > > field to vary between years, just an overall coefficient that will be
> > > applied
> > > to the entire spatial field. Is there a way to implement this in R-INLA?
> > >
> > > Thank you!!
