here, there is one data point while the size of 'idx' is 10

> r=inla(y ~ -1 + f(idx, model="iid", values=1:10), data=data.frame(y=0,idx=1))
> r$summary.random$idx$mean
 [1] 1.579007224e-19 3.875343889e-19 3.875343889e-19 3.875343889e-19
 [5] 3.875343889e-19 3.875343889e-19 3.875343889e-19 3.875343889e-19
 [9] 3.875343889e-19 3.875343889e-19

>

On Mon, 2025-02-24 at 14:52 +0300, Håvard Rue wrote:
> it shouldn't be a problem. 'idx' at
>
> f(idx, model=your.rgeneric)
>
> can still be 1:n while your model 'your.rgeneric' can be of size 2*n
>
>
> On Mon, 2025-02-24 at 10:31 +0100, Leonardo Cefalo wrote:
> > Dear INLA experts
> >
> > In order to fit a multivariate BYM2 model I am trying to implement the
> > sparse
> > parametrization manually. It is possible in theory by using a similar
> > argument
> > to Riebler et al. (2016), i.e. modelling jointly the BYM field and the ICAR
> > component.
> >
> > The problem is that the latent effect length is twice observations length,
> > though only the first half enters the linear predictor.
> >
> > So, before working the multivariate model out, I am trying to implement
> > manually (i.e. with inla.rgeneric.define() ) the univariate BYM to try
> > replicating roughly what the precompiled f(..., model = "bym2" ) function
> > call
> > does. It is a divide-and-conquer approach.
> >
> > However, I cannot find a way to fit a model where the latent effect is twice
> > the size of observations. I tried inla.stack() as with the SPDE model, where
> > the latent field has indeed a higher dimension, but in this case INLA
> > crashes
> > and I cannot figure out the exact problem from the log.
> > Could somebody please help me setting a latent effect of size 2n?
> >
> > Thank you very much. I attach an application example
> > Best,
